{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The card that stayed white in dark mode, and the 40 selectors that fixed one of it</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team added dark mode the fast way: ship the light-themed app, then go component by component adding a <code>.dark .card { background: #1e1e1e; }</code> override for anything that still looked wrong. It worked, sort of. Three weeks later a designer filed a bug: a pricing card nested inside a modal nested inside a dashboard widget was still rendering white-on-white in dark mode, because the override that would have fixed it targeted <code>.dark .card</code>, and this particular card was rendered by a component that used a different class name.
    </p>
    <p>
      By then there were over 40 dark-mode-specific override rules scattered across a dozen files, and nobody could say with confidence whether they covered every component or just the ones someone had happened to notice were broken.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Hardcoded colors plus reactive overrides is an approach with no ceiling on how it fails — every new component is a new place a dark-mode override might be missing, and the override list only grows, it never gets structurally smaller. There's no single place to look to answer "is this themed correctly," only a growing list of patches applied after the fact.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The actual defect wasn't the missing 41st override, it was treating color as a per-component implementation detail instead of a themeable value with a single source of truth. Every hardcoded hex value is a value that has to be found and duplicated for every theme a product ever ships — a burden that compounds linearly with both component count and theme count, which is exactly the kind of growth a design system exists to prevent.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Name the role, not the color, and redefine it once per theme</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A design token is a CSS custom property that names what a color is <em>for</em>, not what it looks like — <code>--color-surface</code> instead of <code>#ffffff</code>, <code>--color-ink</code> instead of <code>#111111</code>. Components reference the token (<code>background: var(--color-surface)</code>), never the raw value. A theme is just a different set of values assigned to those same token names, defined once at the root.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Switching themes becomes swapping which value block is active — light theme sets <code>--color-surface: #ffffff</code> at <code>:root</code>, a <code>[data-theme="dark"]</code> selector (or a <code>prefers-color-scheme: dark</code> media query) redefines the same token to <code>#1e1e1e</code>. Every component that already referenced <code>var(--color-surface)</code> updates automatically — zero component code changes, because the component never knew a specific color, only a role.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is an inversion of where the theming decision lives: with hardcoded colors, "what should this look like in dark mode" is a question asked and answered separately for every component that has one. With tokens, it's asked and answered exactly once, at the theme root, and every consumer inherits the answer through normal CSS cascade and inheritance — the same mechanism that already makes CSS scale, applied to color instead of fought against.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The design system migration that took an afternoon instead of a sprint</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      After the pricing-card incident, the team spent a sprint doing a real token migration: audit every hardcoded color in the codebase, map each one to a semantic token name (<code>--color-surface</code>, <code>--color-surface-raised</code>, <code>--color-border</code>, <code>--color-ink</code>, <code>--color-ink-muted</code>, <code>--color-accent</code>), replace every raw hex value with the matching <code>var()</code> reference, then delete all 40-plus dark-mode override rules — they were no longer needed, because every component now read from a token that dark mode already redefined.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Six months later, the design team wanted to add a third theme — a high-contrast mode for accessibility. Under the old override-per-component approach, that would have meant writing a second parallel set of 40-plus overrides. Under the token approach, it was one new value block at the root defining the same eight token names with high-contrast values. No component code touched. It shipped in an afternoon.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The token layer also caught a category of bug the override approach structurally couldn't: a few components turned out to be using <code>--color-ink</code> (primary text) where they actually needed <code>--color-ink-muted</code> (secondary text) — a distinction that was invisible in light mode because both values happened to have enough contrast against white, but became a real legibility problem once dark mode's much narrower contrast range exposed the difference. Naming the role forced someone to pick the correct role, which naming nothing (just a hex value) never had.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

