{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Tokens are still a naming discipline, not a magic fix</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Naming matters: <code>--color-surface</code> (what role it plays) ages much better than <code>--color-gray-100</code> (what it currently looks like) — a gray that becomes the "surface" color in light mode might need to become a dark navy in dark mode, and a token named after its role can make that jump cleanly while one named after its current appearance reads as a contradiction.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A two-layer token system scales further: primitive tokens (<code>--gray-900: #111</code>) hold raw values, semantic tokens (<code>--color-ink: var(--gray-900)</code>) alias them to a role, and components only ever reference the semantic layer. This lets a theme remap which primitive backs a given semantic token without renaming anything components already depend on.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Watch inheritance and specificity: a token redefined on a scoped element (say, a themed sidebar nested inside a page using the opposite theme) cascades correctly to its descendants by default, since custom properties inherit like any other CSS property — but a component that reads the token via JS (<code>getComputedStyle</code>) rather than through CSS needs to re-read it on theme change, since JS doesn't get cascade updates for free the way CSS declarations do. The newer <code>light-dark()</code> CSS function and <code>@property</code> (for typed, non-inheriting custom properties) are worth knowing for cases where you want a token to explicitly *not* inherit or want both theme values expressed in one declaration.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

